{
  "version": 1,
  "summary": "Adds verilex-agent, a launcher in its own agent/ module that runs the brain in a sandbox and returns the verdict of a run that verilex itself planned, under one written verdict rule, with a CI check that keeps harness code out of the core.",
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "agent/internal/verdict/verdict.go",
      "summary": "Owns the verdict rule: README \"Verdict rule\" rows 9-30 turn the runs that verilex planned into one verdict, and every other run into a note.",
      "annotations": [
        {
          "newRange": [
            60,
            67
          ],
          "summary": "Request reads requested.chain, the field verilex fills with the caller's chain, or null when verilex planned the chain.",
          "rationale": "F8: the launcher used to parse the brain's arguments to tell a chain from a planned run, and read an empty chain argument differently from verilex, so a planned red stopped being final. Now verilex is the one owner of that reading. Verified by row 11 of TestVerdictRule (an empty chain argument, then a green, stays red) and by TestRequestNamesTheCallersChain in the core.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            83,
            109
          ],
          "summary": "Parse refuses a claim run whose requested has no chain key, so an older verilex fails closed.",
          "rationale": "Without the key, every chain run given --changed would read as planned, which is the F9 false green. The check reads key presence, not value, because null is the planned value. Verified by TestClaimRunWithoutRequestedChainIsInconclusive and by the mutation that skips the check.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            121,
            188
          ],
          "summary": "Decide keeps only planned runs for the verdict: the last planned red is final, then the budget, then no planned run, then the last planned run.",
          "rationale": "MAIN narrowed the rule after four checks found holes in how brain chains counted (F7, F9, F10). A brain chain can be red on correct code or green on inputs that miss a defect, so it never decides and never proves a claim; it becomes a note. Verified by TestVerdictRule rows 9-30 and by mutations: every run planned, a chain with --changed planned, no final planned red, no budget step, no note, no spec check and no open claims each fail named rows.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            190,
            203
          ],
          "summary": "isPlanned is verilex's own reading of a run, and note is the one-line report for a run that is not planned.",
          "rationale": "isPlanned needs the claim-run format, a requested object and a null chain, so a plain chain run (no format) and a chain given --changed (chain set) are both brain chains. Verified by rows 16, 17, 19 and 20, each of which also checks the note count.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            252,
            277
          ],
          "summary": "check refuses a planned green that did not prove a floor claim or was not asked about a changed path.",
          "rationale": "The old not-a-claim-run clause is gone, because every planned run is a claim run. Rows 24, 25 and 27 cover each reason, and the mutation that skips check fails exactly those rows.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "path": "agent/internal/run/run.go",
      "summary": "One launcher invocation: resolve the spec and its claim floor, snapshot the project, run the brain in the sandbox, check the project and home, return verilex's verdict.",
      "annotations": [
        {
          "newRange": [
            35,
            44
          ],
          "summary": "Result carries Notes, the launcher's report on each brain chain, also when the verdict rule ends inconclusive.",
          "rationale": "A brain chain never decides, but its red can point at a real defect that only the chain reached, so the launcher reports it instead of dropping it. Verified by rows 16, 17, 19 and 20 of TestVerdictRule.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            156,
            165
          ],
          "summary": "One deadline now covers the intent lookup as well as both brain phases, and floor runs inside it.",
          "rationale": "N5: a hung verilex index used to hold the launcher with no limit, and the budget started only after it. Verified by row 4 of TestVerdictRule (a 1s budget ends a hung index in about one second) and the Lookup failures recipe.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            238,
            258
          ],
          "summary": "The launcher hands every run the proxy kept, and whether the budget ended, to verdict.Decide, and returns its verdict and notes.",
          "rationale": "The rule has one owner. Rows 1-8 (spec, floor, sandbox, F1 and F5 checks) stay in Run, because they apply before any run is judged. With no planned run and a failed brain, the reason names both.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            343,
            373
          ],
          "summary": "newLaunch builds the hide list: both homes, the ledger, the verilex binary, VERILEX_HOME, VERILEX_LEDGER, the run's logs and its verilex home.",
          "rationale": "These decide a verdict, so the brain must neither read nor write them. The ledger directory is created before the brain starts so the sandbox can hide it. Verified by the forgery test: every pass added to the ledger names a run the launcher made, and every earlier file is unchanged.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            388,
            438
          ],
          "summary": "brain passes the prompt as a file on stdin and writes the brain's output to the run's log files.",
          "rationale": "With no pipe, no copy goroutine can wait on a child the brain left running, which matters on macOS, where a detached process can outlive the run. Verified by TestBudgetBoundsTheRunWhateverTheBrainStarts (a setsid child) and the stdin check in TestSkillIntentAndSuggestionsReachTheBrain; a prompt larger than the pipe buffer is not tested.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            522,
            546
          ],
          "summary": "resolve gives verilex ticket at most 30 seconds, and a timeout is inconclusive.",
          "rationale": "N5 also named the ticket lookup. It cannot use the time budget, because the budget comes from the ticket. Verified on the real launcher by the Lookup failures recipe: a hung ticket ends after about 30 seconds with 'verilex ticket did not answer within 30s'. Not in the agent tests, because the test would wait 30 seconds.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            548,
            582
          ],
          "summary": "floor asks verilex index for the intent's claims within the budget, and a failure, a timeout, or no claim without --claim is inconclusive.",
          "rationale": "N6: an index failure is the environment's, so it says inconclusive, not refused. The floor must come from verilex, not from the brain. Verified by rows 1, 3, 4 and 26 of TestVerdictRule, and by the mutations that return the failure as a refusal (row 3 fails) or drop the budget (row 4 fails).",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            588,
            616
          ],
          "summary": "verilexJSON runs each lookup in its own process group with a 30-second deadline, and kills the group when the deadline or the budget ends.",
          "rationale": "If the deadline killed only the parent, a child that it started would keep the pipes open and hold the launcher; killing the group ends both, and WaitDelay bounds the wait for the pipes. Verified by the Lookup failures recipe: after both hangs, pgrep finds no hung lookup left.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        }
      ]
    },
